            "id": "4674394",
            "title": "3 things to look out for on Thursday",
            "description": "<html><body><p data-eci=\"true\">Wall Street's major averages finished mixed on Wednesday after Nvidia reported better-than-expected Q3 results amid the recent CEO scandal. The Nasdaq ended higher even as Nvidia closed lower.</p>\n<p>The benchmark S&amp;P 500 (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/SP500\" title=\"S&amp;P 500 Index\">SP500</a>) finished -0.2%, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/COMP:IND\" title=\"NASDAQ Composite Index\">COMP:IND</a>) ended +0.4, and the blue-chip Dow (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/DJI\" title=\"Dow Jones Industrial Average Index\">DJI</a>) finished -1.1%.</p>\n<p>Now, here are three things to look out for on Thursday:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Take-Two Interactive's (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/TTWO\" title=\"Take-Two Interactive Software, Inc.\">TTWO</a>) <em>Grand Theft Auto 6</em> will be officially released about 13 years since GTA 5 hit the market. The highly-anticipated video game will be natively available on Sony's (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/SONY\" title=\"Sony Group Corporation\">SONY</a>) PlayStation 5 and PS5 Pro, as well as Microsoft's (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/MSFT\" title=\"Microsoft Corporation\">MSFT</a>) Xbox Series X|S.</li>\n<li>When Alibaba (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/BABA\" title=\"Alibaba Group Holding Limited\">BABA</a>) reported second-quarter earnings before the opening bell, investors will be keenly watching for the firm’s AI- and cloud-related revenue and its capital expenditure. Earnings are expected to more than double this quarter to $1.65 per share, due to a weak last-year comparison. The top line is projected to see a 16% Y/Y jump to $40.49B.</li>\n<li>Walmart (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/WMT\" title=\"Walmart Inc.\">WMT</a>) is also set to release its fiscal Q3 earnings results before market open, with Wall Street, on average, expecting the retail giant to post a quarterly EPS of $0.64 on revenue of $187.25B.</li>\n</ul>\n</body></html>",
